  "problem": {
    "statement": "Solve x^2 + 10x + 7 = 0 by completing the square.",
    "givens": [
      "x^2 + 10x + 7 = 0"
    ],
    "unknown": "the values of x",
    "assessment": {
      "answer": "x = -5 ± 3√2",
      "answer_value": "-5 ± 3√2",
      "rationale": "Half of 10 is 5; add 25 to both sides; (x+5)^2 = 18."
    }
  },
  "problem_chapter_index": 3,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "why-bother",
      "role": "why",
      "title": "Why would anyone cut a quadratic into pieces?",
      "duration_hint_s": 40,
      "beats": [
        {
          "id": "why-1",
          "narration": {
            "text": "Here's a funny thing about quadratics. Some of them factor in a blink, and some just refuse. But there's one trick that cracks every single one of them, and it starts with a square you can hold in your hands.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "show-square",
                "word_index": 31
              }
            ],
            "pause_ms": 400,
            "intent": "spark curiosity"
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "geometry_figure",
              "id": "unit-square",
              "props": {
                "shape": "square",
                "side_label": "x"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "center",
                "emphasis": "high"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "show-square"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "babylon",
      "role": "history",
      "title": "Babylon, 1800 BCE: the clay-tablet geometers",
      "skippable": true,
      "duration_hint_s": 55,
      "beats": [
        {
          "id": "babylon-1",
          "narration": {
            "text": "Almost four thousand years ago, scribes in Babylon pressed little wedges into wet clay and solved problems about fields. They didn't have the letter x, or even negative numbers. What they had was a pair of scissors, so to speak. They'd cut a rectangle, slide a piece around, and turn the puzzle into a square.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "tablet",
                "word_index": 4
              },
              {
                "id": "cut",
                "word_index": 38
              }
            ],
            "pause_ms": 500,
            "intent": "tell the human story"
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "id": "tablet",
              "props": {
                "kind": "clay-tablet",
                "caption": "A Babylonian field problem"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "left"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "tablet"
            },
            {
              "type": "geometry_figure",
              "id": "cut-rect",
              "props": {
                "shape": "rectangle",
                "split": "half-the-width"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "right"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "cut"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-corner",
      "role": "seek",
      "title": "What's the missing corner?",
      "duration_hint_s": 60,
      "beats": [
        {
          "id": "seek-1",
          "narration": {
            "text": "Take x squared plus six x. Draw a big square for the x squared, and a long strip for the six x. Now split that strip in half and wrap the halves around two sides of the square. Look at the picture. What's missing? A little corner. A three by three corner, nine whole units.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "big-square",
                "word_index": 6
              },
              {
                "id": "strips",
                "word_index": 18
              },
              {
                "id": "corner",
                "word_index": 42
              }
            ],
            "pause_ms": 300,
            "intent": "guide discovery"
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "geometry_figure",
              "id": "area-model",
              "props": {
                "shape": "square-with-strips",
                "square": "x",
                "strip_widths": [
                  "3",
                  "3"
                ],
                "missing_corner": "3 by 3"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "center",
                "emphasis": "high"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "strips"
            },
            {
              "type": "brace",
              "id": "half-b",
              "props": {
                "spans": "strip",
                "label": "half of b"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "bottom"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "corner"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "seek-2",
          "narration": {
            "text": "So the secret is always the same: take half of the middle number, square it, and that's the corner you need to add to finish the big square.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "rule",
                "word_index": 14
              }
            ]
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "id": "rule",
              "props": {
                "text": "corner = (b / 2)^2"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "right"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "rule"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "finish-it",
      "role": "how",
      "title": "Finish the square, balance the equation",
      "duration_hint_s": 70,
      "beats": [
        {
          "id": "how-1",
          "narration": {
            "text": "Let's try it on a friendly one: x squared plus six x plus two equals zero. Slide the two across. Add the nine corner to both sides, because whatever we do to one side, we do to the other. Now the left side is a perfect square, x plus three, all squared.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "move",
                "word_index": 11
              },
              {
                "id": "add",
                "word_index": 19
              },
              {
                "id": "square",
                "word_index": 42
              }
            ],
            "pause_ms": 400,
            "intent": "model the method"
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "equation_steps",
              "id": "worked",
              "props": {
                "steps": [
                  "x^2 + 6x + 2 = 0",
                  "x^2 + 6x = -2",
                  "x^2 + 6x + 9 = 7",
                  "(x + 3)^2 = 7"
                ]
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "left"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "move"
            },
            {
              "type": "balance_scale",
              "id": "balance",
              "props": {
                "left": "x^2 + 6x + 9",
                "right": "7",
                "note": "add 9 to both sides"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "right"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "add"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "how-2",
          "narration": {
            "text": "From there it's just a square root away. Take the root of both sides, remember the plus-or-minus, and slide the three back. Two solutions, and both of them land right where the picture promised.",
            "cues": [
              {
                "id": "root",
                "word_index": 8
              }
            ]
          },
          "visuals": [
            {
              "type": "number_line",
              "id": "roots",
              "props": {
                "points": [
                  "-3 - sqrt(7)",
                  "-3 + sqrt(7)"
                ],
                "center": "-3"
              },
              "layout": {
                "region": "bottom"
              },
              "reveal_cue": "root"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "your-turn",
      "role": "check",
